  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nRest of Today - Friday: Cooler, Sprinkles/Flurries Possible\n\nCooler temperatures are on tap through Friday as northerly \nsynoptic flow behind the trough situated over the Great Lakes \nmaintains a cooler airmass over the region. Currently, watching \na shortwave perturbation across Western Ontario that will try to\nsneak down into the region later this evening. As this occurs, \nsome low-level instability with steeper low-level lapse rates in\nthe presence of this forcing may be sufficient for some \nflurries to develop late this afternoon and into the evening. \nHowever, the saturated layer in RAP soundings is fairly shallow \nso this makes confidence low (10% chance) that any precipitation\nwith this wave would manifest. Otherwise, temperatures will \nremain cool into Friday with respectable confidence in the NBM \n(40-90% chance) for highs to remain in the 30s for southeast MN \nand north-central WI and temperatures only rising into the lower\n40s elsewhere. \n\nThis Weekend: Dry, Slight Warming Trend\n\nAs we head into the weekend, upper-level ridging and surface high \npressure builds into the region allow for some very weak southerly \nreturn flow. Consequently, expecting temperatures to slowly begin a \nwarming trend with highs returning into the 40s for much of the area \nover the weekend. Overall given the high pressure in place over the \nweekend, it is unsurprising that the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) has a 0% chance for any measurable \nprecipitation. \n\nNext Week: Mild, Dry to Start the Week with Precipitation Chances \nReturning Late Week\n\nLooking ahead to next week, upper-level ridging and surface high \npressure remains predominately just south to southeast of the \nregion. This will aid in maintaining southerly flow through the \nearly part of next week into the local area and thus continue the \nwarming trend through the middle of next week. As a result, the NBM \nhas fairly high probabilities (50-90%) for highs to reach above 50 \ndegrees as soon as Tuesday with even some low-end probabilities\n(10-40%) for highs to hit 60 degrees on Wednesday through next \nFriday. In addition to the warmer temperatures, deterministic \nand ensemble guidance hint at and upper-level trough beginning \nto pivot into the Great Plains by Thursday. As a result, \nexpecting precipitation chances to increase beginning on \nThursday with the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) \nhaving modest probabilities (50-70% chance) for measurable \nprecipitation for Thursday and into Friday. Overall given the \nmuch above normal temperatures anticipated by ensemble guidance,\nwould expect this to be an only rain situation for our local \narea. As we head past next week, the ensemble mean in the \nGEFS/EC ensemble would suggest that some cooler temperatures \nheading into the week of Thanksgiving in the wake of the \naforementioned upper-level trough, however confidence in any \ndetails remains low at this time.",
